// Formative practice quiz for handling-submission-errors-in-forms. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/forms-context-and-crud/handling-submission-errors-in-forms

::handling-submission-errors-in-forms o1 - field error placement::The server returns a 400 saying the email is already taken. Where should that message appear?{
~As a form-level banner only#Not quite. People would have to work out which field it means.
~In the browser console#Not quite. Users do not read the console.
~Nowhere, because the form should clear#Not quite. The person needs to know what to fix.
=Next to the email field#Correct. It is about that field, so show it where it can be acted on.
}

::handling-submission-errors-in-forms o2 - server down::A submission fails because the server returned 500. What should the form do?{
~Clear the form#Not quite. They would have to type it all again.
=Show a form-level message and keep what was typed#Correct. It is not about one field, and the typed text must not be lost.
~Show the message under the first field#Not quite. That falsely blames a field.
~Say nothing and silently retry forever#Not quite. People need to know what is going on, and endless retries add load.
}

::handling-submission-errors-in-forms o2 - when to clear::When should a form clear what the user typed?{
~As soon as Submit is pressed#Not quite. A failure would then lose their work.
~After any response#Not quite. A failed response should keep their text.
=Only after a successful response#Correct. Only then do you know the data was saved.
~Never, not even after success#Not quite. After success, a cleared form tells them it is done.
}

::handling-submission-errors-in-forms o3 - server is authority::Why handle server validation even when client validation passes?{
=The server is the authority\: requests can bypass the form, and some rules are only known there#Correct. For example, a taken email can only be checked on the server.
~Client validation never runs in production#Not quite. It does run. It just is not the authority.
~Because the browser ignores JavaScript forms#Not quite. That is not true.
~You do not need to\: client validation is enough#Not quite. Client checks can be bypassed, and cannot know server-side rules.
}
